 # Ray Traversal Algorithms
 
-Also called: Octree Ray Traversal, Hierarchical Ray Marching
+Also called: Octree Ray Traversal, Sparse Voxel Octree Traversal, Hierarchical Ray Marching
 
 ## Overview
-Ray Traversal Algorithms are a family of hierarchical descent methods used to efficiently locate occupied voxels or isosurfaces within sparse volumetric datasets stored in octrees or similar tree structures. They solve the problem of avoiding redundant intersection tests in empty space by skipping subtrees that do not intersect the viewing ray. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [GPU Octree Volume Rasterization](5209-gpu-octree-volume-rasterization-with-shadow-mapping.md) by its explicit use of ray-tree intersection logic (slab tests, DDA, or neighbor finding) rather than rasterization primitives, and from [Camera-Driven Octree Subdivision](7636-camera-driven-octree-subdivision.md) by its focus on per-ray traversal state rather than global view-dependent subdivision.
+Ray Traversal Algorithms are a family of hierarchical descent methods used to efficiently determine which nodes of a sparse voxel octree (SVO) or similar tree structure intersect a given ray. They solve the problem of avoiding redundant intersection tests in large, sparse volumetric datasets by skipping empty subtrees and jumping directly to occupied leaf nodes or boundary cells. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [GPU Octree Volume Rasterization](5209-gpu-octree-volume-rasterization-with-shadow-mapping.md) by its reliance on explicit ray-tree intersection logic rather than rasterization primitives, and from [Camera-Driven Octree Subdivision](7636-camera-driven-octree-subdivision.md) by its focus on per-ray traversal state rather than global view-dependent subdivision.
 
