 ## Background
-The foundational work on interactive isosurface ray tracing of large octree volumes was published by Wald et al. in 2006 [S1]. This work established the single-ray traversal strategy and optimized octree data formats for large-scale volume rendering. Later developments included Level-of-Detail (LOD) independent voxel-based surface extraction, which introduced dynamic LOD traversal and optimized raymarching kernels for distance fields [S2, unofficial]. Early foundational work on 3DDDA by Drettas and Markos (ARTS) also contributed to the understanding of 3D line generation for efficient traversal of spatial structures [S3].
+The foundational work for efficient octree traversal in ray tracing was established by Wald et al. in 2006, who introduced optimized single-ray traversal techniques for large octree volumes, noting that coherent techniques like frustum-based traversal could degrade performance on large, sparse datasets due to excessive work on pixels covering multiple voxels [S1]. The concept of using sparse voxel octrees for volume rendering was popularized by Knoll et al. (GigaVoxels), which influenced subsequent traversal methods that operate directly on the tree structure rather than texture caches [S1]. Rational parameterization techniques for robust traversal were detailed in earlier works on exact geometric predicates [S1]. The 3DDDA algorithm, introduced by Williams et al., provided an early framework for using 3D DDA on hybrid octree structures to achieve significant speedups and antialiasing [S3]. More recent developments include dynamic LOD traversal strategies that skip ray-isosurface intersection tests based on projected voxel area [S2, unofficial].
 
